@page "/progress"

<PageTitle>Progress – ESIR</PageTitle>

<PageHeader Title="Progress" Mood="neutral">
    <p>
        <strong>We are working on Version 1 (V1 – Core).</strong>
        ESIR is in development, so we only mark something as <strong>Done</strong> when it really works.
        This page is more technical: it shows how ESIR is built and how far we have come.
    </p>
</PageHeader>

<section>
    <h2>The three versions</h2>
    <div class="versions">
        <div class="version version--now">
            <h3>V1 – Core <span class="here">we are here</span></h3>
            <p>The first working ESIR: a robot you can touch, and a website where you pick how you feel. The website shows what the robot has confirmed.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="version">
            <h3>V2 – Improve</h3>
            <p>Finer gestures, eye colour and brightness, public pages and automatic deployment.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="version">
            <h3>V3 – Optional</h3>
            <p>A body that moves its head, and a microphone, only after privacy checks.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Version 1 in detail</h2>
    <p>
        V1 is the version we must finish. It has to work from start to finish: you touch or tilt ESIR, or pick a feeling
        on the website, and ESIR answers with a face and light, while the website shows what the robot has confirmed.
    </p>

    <h3>Our plan for V1</h3>
    <p>In our pitch (18 November 2026) we planned to get V1 working in about two and a half weeks, in three steps.</p>
    <ol class="plan">
        @foreach (var step in _plan)
        {
            <li><strong>@step.Title</strong> @step.Text</li>
        }
    </ol>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>What V1 must do</h2>
    <p>
        <strong>@_done of @_total parts done, @_inProgress in progress, @_planned planned.</strong>
        A part is "in progress" when we have started building it.
    </p>
    <div class="meter" role="img" aria-label="@_done done, @_inProgress in progress, @_planned planned">
        <span style="width: @(100 * _done / _total)%"></span>
        <span style="width: @(100 * _inProgress / _total)%"></span>
    </div>
    <ul class="tasks">
        @foreach (var part in _must)
        {
            <li>
                <span class="part"><strong>@part.Title</strong><span class="detail">@part.Detail</span></span>
                <span class="badge @BadgeClass(part.Status)">@part.Status</span>
            </li>
        }
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>What V1 needs, layer by layer</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Layer</th><th scope="col">What V1 needs</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in _layers)
                {
                    <tr><th scope="row">@row.Name</th><td>@row.Needs</td></tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>V1 is done when…</h2>
    <p>We call V1 finished only when all of these are true. None of them is ticked yet.</p>
    <ul class="checks">
        @foreach (var item in _doneWhen)
        {
            <li>@item</li>
        }
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>What V1 does not include</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
        <li>Crisis monitoring, diagnosis or treatment claims. ESIR is a companion, not a therapist.</li>
        <li>Storing conversations or emotion history.</li>
        <li>Free navigation or advice from an AI without limits.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>How the robot reacts</h2>
    <p>ESIR has touch pads and a motion sensor. This is what each action should do. We have not tuned any of it on the real robot yet.</p>
    <div class="reactions">
        @foreach (var r in _reactions)
        {
            <article class="reaction">
                <RobotFace Mood="@r.Mood" Size="4.5px" />
                <h3>@r.Trigger</h3>
                <p>@r.What</p>
                <p class="sensor">@r.Sensor</p>
            </article>
        }
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Six feelings, two answers</h2>
    <p>
        When you pick a feeling, ESIR answers twice: on the robot and on the website. Today the website shows ESIR's face
        and a kind message. The rest of the website answers, and everything on the robot, are still being built.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">Feeling</th><th scope="col">On the robot (planned)</th><th scope="col">On the website</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                @foreach (var row in _feelings)
                {
                    <tr><th scope="row">@row.Feeling</th><td>@row.Robot</td><td>@row.Website</td></tr>
                }
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>The body</h2>
    <p>The board sits inside a 3D-printed shell that we design in CAD. Both ideas below are concepts, and the fit still has to be checked.</p>
    <div class="two">
        <div class="box box--now">
            <h3>Version 1: a fixed disc</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>A flat disc shell, with the board between two halves.</li>
                <li>A window for the display.</li>
                <li>Open touch pads, so you can reach them.</li>
                <li>USB access and a removable back.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="box box--soon">
            <h3>Version 3 idea: a body that moves</h3>
            <ul>
                <li>A head and arms moved by small motors (servos), on the same board mount.</li>
                <li>We would start with one head movement only.</li>
                <li>This only happens if there is time.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
    <figure class="concepts">
        <img src="images/esir-concepts.jpg" alt="Four ESIR enclosure concepts: a mint cat, a classic blue robot, a peach box and a lilac pebble." loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption>Four shell concepts to choose from: a mint cat, a classic robot, a peach box and a lilac pebble. Board size, touch openings and fit still need to be checked.</figcaption>
    </figure>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Safe by design</h2>
    <ul class="plain">
        <li><strong>Stop always wins.</strong> It cancels whatever ESIR is doing and returns it to quiet rest. You can stop it with a touch pad or from the website.</li>
        <li><strong>Limits on lights and sound,</strong> with a way to switch them off.</li>
        <li><strong>No surprises after a restart.</strong> ESIR starts with no lights or sounds on.</li>
        <li><strong>Old commands are ignored.</strong> Every command has an ID, so a repeated or outdated command is skipped.</li>
        <li><strong>It should keep working without Wi-Fi,</strong> and the website only shows what the robot has confirmed.</li>
        <li><strong>No recordings and no personal data.</strong> You can't talk to ESIR directly yet. A microphone is only a later idea, and only after privacy checks.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>How the robot and the website talk</h2>
    <ol class="flow">
        <li>You pick a feeling on the website. The website puts a command for the robot in a waiting line on the server.</li>
        <li>About once a second the robot asks the server: "Is there something for me?" The server never calls the robot.</li>
        <li>The robot shows the matching reaction and tells the server each step, with the command ID and the status: received, running, completed.</li>
        <li>The website shows only what the robot has confirmed. If the robot is offline, the command waits for a short while and then expires.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>There is no database in the first version. Commands and robot status live in memory, and nothing personal is stored.</p>
</section>

<section>
    <h2>Later versions</h2>
    <div class="two">
        <div class="box box--now">
            <h3>V2 – Improve</h3>
            <ul>
                @foreach (var item in _v2)
                {
                    <li>@item</li>
                }
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="box box--soon">
            <h3>V3 – Optional</h3>
            <ul>
                @foreach (var item in _v3)
                {
                    <li>@item</li>
                }
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="after">We also built something that was not in the plan: the Relax page, with breathing rhythms, a grounding exercise and a body release exercise.</p>
</section>